File origins
------------
Copied from github.com/libdns/wedos @ 3946767 (the security-relevant WAPI
client — auditable by diffing against that commit). BYTE-FOR-BYTE except
request.go, provider.go, convert.go and response.go, which carry the bug fixes
noted below:

  provider.go   request.go   response.go   types.go   convert.go

Local fix in request.go (createAuthString):
  The WAPI auth token hashes `username + sha1(password) + currentHour`. WEDOS
  expects the hour zero-padded to two digits (00-23, Europe/Prague), as the
  documentation ("00-23") and reference clients (e.g. wedos-hook: `date +"%H"`,
  Posh-ACME: `.ToString('HH')`) do. Upstream formatted it with `%d`, dropping
  the leading zero for hours 0-9, so authentication failed every day between
  00:00 and 09:59 Prague time. Changed `%d` to `%02d`.

  NOTE: this requires the runtime to have the Europe/Prague zoneinfo so
  time.LoadLocation succeeds (otherwise it falls back to a fixed UTC+1 and the
  hour is wrong during summer time). The Containerfile installs tzdata for this.

Local fix in convert.go (wedosTTL):
  toWedosDNSRecord rendered the record TTL with int(r.TTL). r.TTL is a
  time.Duration (nanoseconds), so a 300s TTL was sent as "300000000000", which
  WEDOS rejects with code 2317 ("invalid TTL limit") — so no record was ever
  added and ACME DNS-01 got "expected one record, got 0". Added a wedosTTL helper
  that converts the duration to whole seconds and clamps to the WEDOS minimum of
  300 (also covers a zero/unset TTL); applied it to every record type.

Local fix in provider.go (commitDomain + calls):
  WEDOS DNS changes are staged: dns-row-add/update/delete only take effect after
  the zone is published with dns-domain-commit (data {"name": <zone>}). Upstream
  never commits, so added/changed records never went live and ACME DNS-01
  validation could never see the TXT record. Added a commitDomain helper and
  call it after a successful AppendRecords / SetRecords / DeleteRecords. (This is
  what reference clients like Posh-ACME do via dns-domain-commit.)

Local fix in provider.go (DeleteRecords matching):
  Upstream matched rows to delete on name+type+rdata and removed each match key
  after a single hit, so at most one row per (name,type,value) was deleted and
  any value-representation difference left the row in place. Challenge TXT
  records therefore accumulated; Let's Encrypt's multi-perspective validation
  then matched a stale token and failed ("Incorrect TXT record ... found",
  including "During secondary validation"). Changed DeleteRecords to match on
  name+type only and delete EVERY matching row, which reliably clears the
  _acme-challenge TXT records (and any stale leftovers). Safe here because the
  provider is used only for ACME and the persistent records are A records (a
  different type).

  LIMITATION: because it ignores rdata, this would also remove a legitimate
  co-located TXT of the same name, and it would break a single cert that needs
  two simultaneous _acme-challenge values (a wildcard + apex SAN). Neither
  applies to this single, non-wildcard domain. Revisit if that changes.

Local fix in provider.go (trailing-dot zone in the "domain" payload):
  libdns passes the zone in FQDN form with a trailing dot (e.g. "pacmag.cz."), but
  WEDOS rejects that on dns-rows-list with "Invalid or unsupported domain name
  format" (code 2202). commitDomain and toWedosDNSRecord already trimmed it, so
  AppendRecords (presenting the TXT) worked — but GetRecords/SetRecords/DeleteRecords
  passed the raw `zone` straight into {"domain": zone}, so every list/delete failed.
  The practical effect: ACME *cleanup* never ran, so the DeleteRecords orphan-clearing
  logic above was dead code, _acme-challenge TXT tokens piled up, and Let's Encrypt
  eventually matched a stale token and returned 403 "Incorrect TXT record ... found".
  This surfaced only when a domain was challenged more than once (e.g. a staging then
  production attempt left two tokens); a name issued once just left one harmless
  orphan. Added `zone = strings.TrimSuffix(zone, ".")` at the top of GetRecords,
  SetRecords and DeleteRecords.

Local fix in response.go (parseResponse):
  Upstream only returned an error on a JSON-decode failure and returned the
  envelope with no error for any WAPI result code, so a rejected request (auth
  failure, invalid TTL, commit rejection, ...) was silently treated as success
  and surfaced much later as an opaque ACME propagation timeout. parseResponse
  now returns an error when Response.Code != 1000. The redundant per-call
  `Code == 1000` checks in AppendRecords/SetRecords were removed accordingly.

Local fix in convert.go (toLibDNSRecord SRV case):
  The SRV branch indexed the split record name blindly (service[0][1:],
  service[1][1:], service[2]), panicking on a name with fewer than three labels
  or an empty label. Added a length/empty guard that returns an error instead.

KNOWN UPSTREAM LIMITATION (not fixed — not exercised by ACME DNS-01):
  SetRecords keys existing rows by name+type only, so multiple rows sharing a
  name+type collapse to the last one and the libdns "RRset == exactly the input"
  contract isn't honored. certmagic uses AppendRecords/DeleteRecords (not
  SetRecords) for DNS-01, so this doesn't affect this deployment.

Adapted from github.com/caddy-dns/wedos @ 74e426d:

  wedos.go

Edits applied to wedos.go (so the wrapper lives in the same package as the
vendored libdns provider, instead of importing github.com/libdns/wedos):

  1. Removed the `github.com/libdns/wedos` import.
  2. Renamed the wrapper type `Provider` -> `CaddyProvider` to avoid colliding
     with the libdns `Provider` now in this same package. Updated its method
     receivers, the CaddyModule New func, and the interface-guard vars.
  3. Replaced `wedos.Provider` / `*wedos.Provider` references with the local
     `Provider` / `*Provider` (the embedded field is still reached as
     `p.Provider`).

types.go is byte-identical to upstream. provider.go, request.go, convert.go and
response.go carry only the fixes documented above — to re-audit, diff them
against the upstream commit and confirm the only deltas are those edits.

Updating
--------
To bump to a newer upstream, re-fetch the five client files verbatim at the new
commit, re-apply the documented fixes (request.go hour, convert.go wedosTTL +
SRV guard, provider.go commit + DeleteRecords + trailing-dot zone trim,
response.go error surfacing) and the wedos.go merge edits, and update the SHAs
above.
